===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|mechanical engineering}} The [[ratio]] of the [[output]] [[force]] produced by a [[machine]] (especially a [[simple machine]]) to the applied [[input]] force.
#* '''1825''', {{w|Maria Edgeworth}}, {{w|Richard Lovell Edgeworth}}, ''Works of Maria Edgeworth, Volume 1: {{w|Practical Education}}'', Samuel H. Parker, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=CTk1AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA304&dq=%22the+mechanical+advantage+of+the+wedge%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwj8r9_bqtj7AhUazjgGHTfnACIQ6AF6BAhQEAI#v=onepage&q=%22the%20mechanical%20advantage%20of%20the%20wedge%22&f=false page 304],
#*: {{...}}and it will always be found, that the '''mechanical advantage''' of the wedge may be ascertained by comparing its perpendicular elevation with its base.
#* '''1965''', A. E. E. McKenzie, ''General Physics'', {{w|Cambridge University Press}}, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=1hI9AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA76&dq=%22mechanical+advantage%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiny-6Hktr7AhUM8DgGHUefCfYQ6AF6BAhVEAI#v=onepage&q=%22mechanical%20advantage%22&f=false page 76],
#*: In the case of a lever with a '''mechanical advantage''' of 10 the effort has to move ten times as far as the load. This is the price which must always be paid for a '''mechanical advantage'''. Any machine which is designed to have a high '''mechanical advantage''' must also have a high velocity ratio. In fact, 'what is gained in force is lost in speed'.
#* '''2005''', Robert Michael Boyce, Arnold Masterman, ''Plumbing: A Practical Guide for Level 2'', Nelson Thornes, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=aAwtOuDupowC&pg=PA131&dq=%22Mechanical+advantage+is+a+ratio+of+like+quantities+and+therefore+has+no+units%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwj9wK75jtr7AhXxpOkKHV5_AlQQ6AF6BAgHEAI#v=onepage&q=%22Mechanical%20advantage%20is%20a%20ratio%20of%20like%20quantities%20and%20therefore%20has%20no%20units%22&f=false page 131],
#*: '''Mechanical advantage''' is a ratio of like quantities and therefore has no units.

